
# Beads / Dolt files (added by bd init)
.dolt/
*.db
# A SQLite database is up to three files: the `-shm` and `-wal` siblings are written next to it
# and are NOT matched by `*.db`, because the name ends in `-shm` or `-wal`. The spike harness
# left 28 of them untracked and unignored, which made `git status` noisy enough to skim past --
# and a dirty-looking tree nobody reads is how a real untracked file gets missed. Same hazard as
# `*.db` below: this would also ignore a committed `-wal` fixture, and the same negation applies.
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
.beads-credential-key
.beads/proxieddb/

# The beads issue-change log: an append-only export of this checkout's issue history, the same
# kind of artifact as `.beads/issues.jsonl`, which bd already treats as local. It is not the
# product, and it grows on every `bd` command, so a repo that tracks it puts a diff in every
# commit that touches an issue.
.beads/interactions.jsonl

# Node
node_modules/

# Tooling caches
.mast/

# ascend corpus (local-only, never committed)
.ascend/

# align machine-local files — telemetry (opt-in: ALIGN_TELEMETRY=1 / --telemetry / align.config.ts telemetry:true)
# and the scan-observation record, which is evidence about THIS checkout only (ADR 029).
.align/telemetry.jsonl
.align/telemetry-state.json
.align/last-scan.json
.align/.lock

# OS cruft. No global core.excludesFile is configured in this checkout, so nothing
# else would catch these — macOS writes .DS_Store without being asked.
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
._*

# Editor swap and backup files.
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Editor project state. The agent-tool directories (.claude/, .codex/, .agents/) are
# deliberately TRACKED — they are committed configuration, not local state. If shared
# VS Code or JetBrains settings are ever wanted, add a negation for them here.
.vscode/
.idea/

# What a skill WROTE, as opposed to what it IS. `.agents/skills/` is configuration and stays
# tracked; this is a report about one codebase at one moment, which is evidence about a session
# rather than part of the product — the same reason `.align/`'s scan record is ignored above.
.agents/research/

# Build and test output.
#
# `dist/` was tracked until 2026-09-11 (69 files across 5 packages). Every package's
# `exports` points at it, so the built output was committed to let the CLI run from a
# clone with no build step — but it meant every source change dragged 69 generated files
# through the diff, and `pnpm clean` deleted tracked files and dirtied the tree.
#
# Cost of untracking, and how it is paid: the CLI needs a build in a fresh clone, and the
# `prepare` script in the root package.json supplies it. Measured 2026-09-12: `pnpm install`
# with every dist tree deleted runs `prepare` -> `tsc -b` and rebuilds all five packages.
# The test suite does NOT need it either way (measured: 278 tests pass with every dist tree
# deleted, because vitest resolves package source), so what `prepare` buys is `asc` itself.
#
# Two ways it does not fire, neither silent: `--ignore-scripts` (which also skips the native
# build of any dependency shipping one) and a pnpm invocation that has nothing to install.
packages/*/dist/
coverage/
.eslintcache

# Logs.
*.log
npm-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A hazard in the beads block above, documented rather than changed.
#
# `*.db` is what keeps the spike's generated databases out of git (spike/corpus.db,
# spike/tmp/*.db — verified ignored). It is global, so it would also silently ignore a
# .db TEST FIXTURE. That failure is invisible here and shows up as a test passing
# locally and failing in a fresh clone, with nothing pointing at the cause. If a
# committed fixture is ever needed, negate it explicitly:
#
#   !packages/store/test/fixtures/**/*.db
#
# Not added speculatively: no such fixture exists yet, and a negation for a directory
# that is not there is a rule nobody can verify.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The spike's raw dumps, by directory rather than by extension.
#
# The harnesses write whole transcripts, whole prompts and whole entry documents into
# spike/tmp/ — the content itself, not a summary of it. That content is real: it is
# other people's recorded work, and it is what the measurements above are measuring.
# This repository is public, so a stray `git add -A` would publish it, and the earlier
# `*.db` rule would not have stopped it because the dumps are `.json` and `.txt`.
#
# Ignoring the DIRECTORY, not the extensions, is the point: the next harness will write
# a fifth extension and should not have to remember this. The harnesses regenerate
# everything under here from `~/.claude/projects` and the store, so nothing in it is
# the only copy of anything. `spike/` itself stays tracked — eslint.config.js keeps it
# precisely because re-litigating a measured decision requires re-running it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
spike/tmp/
